# EvidenceGate — when should a self-improving agent keep its own skill edit?

A self-improving tool-using agent can rewrite its own operating procedure: it fails, proposes an edit to its skill file (`SKILL.md`), and keeps the edit if an acceptance rule says the edit helped. EvidenceGate is a testbed for that acceptance rule. In a deterministic synthetic incident-response world where every episode is graded against a hidden root cause, we froze a pool of {{design.proposer_candidates}} self-proposed edits and {{design.control_candidates}} hand-written controls, pre-registered four acceptance gates and the metrics that judge them, and then re-scored every edit on held-out incidents. Table T1 lists the verdict for every pre-registered question. The exploratory findings concern edits that help on average while hurting one part of the task: gates that judge the mean admitted them, and a metric with a floor at zero hid one of them.

This README reports results. Every result value in it is generated from the two result files, the run summary, the release manifest, the E1 definition and the design constants (see *Fixed procedure* and *Reproduce*); none was typed by hand.

## Problem

An agent that edits its own skill file needs an admission rule: when is an edit trusted enough to keep? The obvious rules compare the edited skill with the old one on some set of tasks and keep the edit if it does better. These rules can fail in at least three ways: an edit can look better on the failures it was written from and not elsewhere; it can raise the share of right answers while lowering the share of right answers reached with the required evidence; and it can raise the average while hurting a part of the task distribution. EvidenceGate measures how often each of a few simple and bootstrap-based rules keeps edits that are harmful, unsupported, or harmful only in part of the task distribution.

## Design

### World and agent

- **OpsWorld.** A deterministic incident simulator. Each incident belongs to one family (`bad_deployment`, `dependency_failure`, `traffic_spike`) with a hidden root cause and one correct action (`rollback_deployment`, `observe_only`, `scale_service`). The agent sees the incident only through read-only tools (service metrics, recent deployments, dependency health, traffic) and ends the episode with a single action. Evidence predicates such as `dependency_ruled_out` are computed from the raw tool outputs observed before the action, and each action has a fixed set of required predicates. L2 incidents add family-specific distractors, and L3 incidents combine the two L2 distractors of a family in one incident. The same `(family, seed, level)` gives the same incident and tool outputs, and hidden values do not appear in any tool output.
- **Agent.** A local LLM (`{{design.policy_id}}`, LangGraph tool loop) that follows a skill file. The initial skill S0 is deliberately imperfect: it often picks the right action without the evidence that action requires.
- **Fixed run condition.** One engine, one machine, one policy configuration (`config_id` {{design.config_id}}) and one tool specification for every run. Each session checks these before it starts.

### Two outcomes

- **Correct Disposition (CD):** the chosen action matches the hidden root cause. This is the primary metric.
- **Evidence-Safe (ES):** CD holds *and* the evidence that action requires had been observed before acting.

### Candidate edits

- **Proposer.** An OpenAI model ({{run.t004.model}}) sees only failed S0 episodes from Dev-A and returns one edited skill and an evidence card. Edits are machine-checked (a single change, structure, no grading terms or numbers) and deduplicated.
- **Pool.** {{design.proposer_candidates}} proposer candidates and {{design.control_candidates}} hand-written controls with labels fixed before measurement, frozen with file hashes.

### Splits

| Split | Incidents | Per cell | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Dev-A | {{design.dev_a_size}} | {{design.dev_a_per_cell}} | the proposer's input and gate C1 |
| Dev-B | {{design.dev_b_size}} | {{design.dev_b_per_cell}} | gates C2–C4 and the budget curve |
| Oracle (L1/L2) | {{design.oracle_size}} | {{design.oracle_per_cell}} | after-the-fact scoring (Ticket 005) |
| Oracle (L3) | {{design.l3_oracle_size}} | {{design.l3_oracle_per_cell}} | after-the-fact scoring of the same decisions (Ticket 006) |

L1/L2 cells are family × level; L3 cells are families.

### Acceptance gates

Each gate compares a candidate with S0 on the same incidents (paired), separately for CD and ES.

| Gate | Data | Accept if |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C1 Dev-A greedy | Dev-A | candidate mean > S0 mean (ties rejected) |
| C2 keep-better | Dev-B | mean paired difference ≥ zero |
| C3 noise threshold | Dev-B | mean difference > δ(N), a high quantile of S0-vs-S0 differences (auxiliary) |
| C4 paired bootstrap | Dev-B | lower percentile of a cell-stratified paired bootstrap of the mean difference > zero, and mean difference > δ(N) |

The budget curve recomputes C2–C4 on stratified Dev-B subsamples of N = {{design.budget_ns}} incidents ({{design.n_resample}} resamples each).

### Oracle scoring and metrics

- **Oracle effect estimate.** Each candidate's difference from S0 on the Oracle incidents, with S0 taken as the majority of {{design.s0_oracle_repeats}} runs per incident, a cell-stratified bootstrap interval ({{design.n_boot}} draws) and per-cell differences.
- **Thresholds.** An effect counts as practically relevant beyond ε = {{design.epsilon}}. A per-cell threshold τ also accounts for S0 repeat noise in that cell. A candidate whose interval crosses a decision boundary is *uncertain* for that decision and is counted separately.
- **Metrics per gate:** Harmful Acceptance Rate, Unsupported Acceptance Rate, Useful Acceptance Precision, Expected Oracle Gain, False Rejection Rate and Local Harm Acceptance Rate (accepted edits with some cell estimate below minus τ). Rates come with counts and Clopper–Pearson intervals; with {{design.proposer_candidates}} proposer candidates, comparisons between gates are descriptive and no tests are run.

All verdicts and thresholds use exact rational arithmetic; Clopper–Pearson intervals are reported as floating-point numbers.

