## Exploratory findings

Everything in this section was looked at after the pre-registered results were known. None of it is a test.

### (a) Gates that judge the mean admitted locally harmful edits

*Exploratory reading of T3.* C4 accepted {{t005.metrics.proposer.cd.C4.accepted.count}} proposer candidates on CD; on the L1/L2 oracle {{t005.metrics.proposer.cd.C4.local_harm}} of them had a cell estimate below minus τ, and on the L3 oracle {{t006.local_harm.proposer.cd.C4.l3}} did. A gate that checks the mean difference does not look at cells, so an edit that gains in one family and loses in another can pass it (Figure F2).

![F2 (CD)](writeup/generated/f2_cd.svg)

*F2. Per-cell oracle effect estimates on CD. Left: L1/L2 cells; right: L3 cells. Outlined cells are below minus τ_cell. The ES version is `writeup/generated/f2_es.svg`.*

### (b) Regressions held in one family and the L3 overall estimate

*Exploratory.* We selected candidates with the same rule as the F3 highlight: confirmed harmful on L3, or an L3 overall point estimate below −ε, on CD. Four proposer candidates meet it. cand-015 is confirmed harmful on L3 (overall estimate {{t006.candidates.cand-015.oracle_l3.cd.delta}}), while cand-002, cand-003 and cand-012 are uncertain: their L3 point estimates are below −ε ({{t006.candidates.cand-002.oracle_l3.cd.delta}}, {{t006.candidates.cand-003.oracle_l3.cd.delta}} and {{t006.candidates.cand-012.oracle_l3.cd.delta}}) and their intervals cross −ε. For all four, the L1/L2 regression sat in the bad_deployment cells. On the L1/L2 oracle a single L2 cell carries a share of {{design.l2_cell_share_l12_oracle}} of the incidents, while on the L3 oracle a family carries {{design.family_share_l3_oracle}}, twice as much. We do not call this a distribution shift, because RQ-L3-0 did not confirm that L3 is harder (Figure F3).

![F3](writeup/generated/f3.svg)

*F3. Overall oracle effect estimate on L1/L2 against L3, one point per candidate. Red: confirmed harmful on L3 or L3 point estimate below minus ε. Controls are highlighted by the same rule (open circles).*

E1 approximates the overall CD estimate if L2-like incidents made up each whole family: the mean of the three L2 cell estimates (definition fixed before computing it). It partly explains the change. E1 is closer to the L3 estimate than the L1/L2 estimate is for {{e1.summary.proposer.cd.closer_count}} of {{e1.summary.proposer.cd.n}} proposer candidates, and the mean absolute residual is {{e1.summary.proposer.cd.mean_abs_residual_e1}} for E1 against {{e1.summary.proposer.cd.mean_abs_residual_l12}} for the L1/L2 estimate. Residuals remain, and the effect inside an L3 cell is not the same as in the matching L2 cell: for cand-003 the bad_deployment estimate moved from {{e1.cand-003.cd.family.bad_deployment.l2}} (L2) to {{e1.cand-003.cd.family.bad_deployment.l3}} (L3), and for cand-015 the traffic_spike estimate from {{e1.cand-015.cd.family.traffic_spike.l2}} to {{e1.cand-015.cd.family.traffic_spike.l3}}. E1 is not the effect of cell weights alone, because the L2 cells also differ in difficulty from the L1 cells.

