### (c) A metric with a floor at zero cannot show a regression

*Exploratory.* This is a property of the metric, illustrated by one case. Where S0's ES rate in a cell is zero, a candidate's ES difference there is at least zero, so a regression in that cell cannot appear on ES. S0's ES rate on bad_deployment was {{derived.s0_es_rate.bad_deployment.l12}} on L1/L2 and {{derived.s0_es_rate.bad_deployment.l3}} on L3. cand-015 is confirmed harmful on CD on L3, while its overall L3 ES estimate is {{t006.candidates.cand-015.oracle_l3.es.delta}} (label: {{t006.candidates.cand-015.oracle_l3.es.subset}}) and its L3 bad_deployment ES cell estimate is {{t006.candidates.cand-015.oracle_l3.es.cells.bad_deployment/L3}}; the C4 gate on ES accepted it.

## Limitations

- **Few candidates.** {{design.proposer_candidates}} proposer candidates; every rate moves in large steps and comparisons are descriptive.
- **ES floor.** S0's ES rate on bad_deployment is {{derived.s0_es_rate.bad_deployment.l12}} on L1/L2 and {{derived.s0_es_rate.bad_deployment.l3}} on L3, so ES cannot show a regression in that family; the same holds on traffic_spike ({{derived.s0_es_rate.traffic_spike.l12}} and {{derived.s0_es_rate.traffic_spike.l3}}), where S0's CD rate is also near zero (see exploratory finding c).
- **L3 manipulation not confirmed.** RQ-L3-0: {{t006.rq.RQ_L3_0.all.verdict}}. S0's CD rate barely moved from L1/L2 to L3, so the L3 re-scoring cannot be read as a test under a harder distribution.
- **Shared seeds.** On the Oracle, a candidate shares its LLM seed with S0's repeat zero, which is one of the {{design.s0_oracle_repeats}} majority votes.
- **Noise threshold.** δ(N) resamples without cell stratification, while C4 resamples within cells.
- **Sensitivity analysis.** The skewed Dev-A resampling for RQ2 does not reproduce the actual skew of the proposer's input.
- **Idle time in the L3 run.** {{run.t006.idle.count}} spans of at least a minute with no episode running added up to {{run.t006.idle.total_s}} s. The machine slept after the laptop lid was closed on battery power (confirmed in the macOS power-management log). No episode spanned these spans, no timeout or error was recorded in or around them, and no model reload was recorded.
- **Candidate generation.** The proposer made {{run.t004.calls}} calls; {{run.t004.excluded.duplicate}} candidates were excluded as duplicates by change-set similarity, which can miss duplicates that differ in wording.
- **Review.** The independent review of this write-up is done by a fresh session of the same model, which is not a fully independent reader.

## Related work

Averages can hide regressions in parts of the data. Oakden-Rayner et al. call this hidden stratification: a model with high overall performance can still consistently miss a rare subtype ([CHIL 2020](https://doi.org/10.1145/3368555.3384468)). Sagawa et al. show that models accurate on average can fail on particular groups, and train for the worst group with group DRO ([arXiv:1911.08731](https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.08731)). Work on model updates counts per-sample regressions between versions, the negative flips ([arXiv:2105.03048](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03048); [arXiv:2011.09161](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.09161), CVPR 2021), and for hosted LLM updates, testing by risk category can find regressions that overall metrics miss ([arXiv:2604.27789](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.27789)). The Local Harm metric applies the same concern to an agent's admission of its own edits.

Self-improving agents keep an edit after testing it in some way. The Darwin Gödel Machine validates each self-modification on coding benchmarks ([arXiv:2505.22954](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22954)); GEPA proposes and tests prompt updates and keeps a Pareto frontier of attempts ([arXiv:2507.19457](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.19457)); Harness² edits a harness without ground-truth verifiers ([arXiv:2610.14224](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14224)); VERSE reports that optimizer self-evolution did not help without execution-based verification in a controlled study ([arXiv:2610.02616](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02616)); and Wei et al. separate proposal from admission and derive, under explicit assumptions, an admission rule that uses fresh validation data ([arXiv:2610.18813](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18813)). Yu et al. report that self-evolution can degrade previously acquired capabilities ([arXiv:2605.09315](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.09315)). The closest work, Lin et al. ([arXiv:2609.24663](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24663)), tracks individual capabilities along an evolution stream at frozen checkpoints on held-out episodes and names candidate evaluation and selection as targets for improvement. EvidenceGate studies that selection step on its own: one round, a frozen pool of edits, admission rules fixed before the run, and every admission decision scored against an oracle effect estimate, overall and per cell, and separately for the right decision and for the evidence behind it.

## Future work

- **Cell-level gates.** A gate that also requires every cell estimate on Dev-B to stay above a noise bound derived from S0-vs-S0 calibration runs (not from the oracle's τ), tested on a new candidate pool.
- **A larger, new candidate pool,** so that locally harmful candidates are frequent enough to compare gates.
- **A stronger L3,** one that is confirmed harder for S0 before the gates are re-scored.
- **An external benchmark pilot** to check whether the same failure modes appear outside OpsWorld.

## Reproduce

Requires `Python 3.12` and [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/). The test suite does not call an LLM or the network.

```bash
uv sync
uv run pytest
uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format --check .
uv run mypy
```

The analysis inputs (episode records of every split, the L3 check and the session logs; {{release.file_count}} files, secret scan with {{release.secret_hits}} hits; the proposer's call ledger is not included) will be published as `{{release.archive}}` once it is attached to a GitHub release of this repository. Check and unpack it at the repository root:

```bash
shasum -a 256 {{release.archive}}   # expect {{hash.records_archive}}
tar -xzf {{release.archive}}        # creates runs/
```

The result files can then be checked and the write-up values regenerated:

```bash
shasum -a 256 results/ticket-005/analysis.json results/ticket-006/analysis.json
uv run python -m evidencegate.writeup              # writeup/generated/values.json
uv run python -m evidencegate.writeup_artifacts    # tables and figures
uv run python -m evidencegate.writeup_render render # this README from writeup/README.template
uv run python -m evidencegate.writeup_render check  # numbers, keys, placement, banned phrases
```

Re-running the analyses is possible (`scripts/run_analysis.sh`, `scripts/run_l3_analysis.sh`), but each refuses to overwrite its result. Re-running the policy needs a local [Ollama](https://ollama.com) server with the same model digest; the commands are in `scripts/gate_benchmark.sh` and `scripts/l3_benchmark.sh`. Displayed values are rounded half up from exact fractions, so the last digit can differ from the float formatting in `results/*/analysis.md`.

## Layout

```
src/opsworld/       the simulator: state, generator (L1, L2, L3), tools, predicates, actions, grader
src/evidencegate/   agent policy, runners, proposer, pool, gates, oracle, metrics, analyses, write-up tools
results/            the two analysis results (JSON, sha256, Markdown)
writeup/            README template, inputs (run summary, E1 definition, release manifest), generated values, tables and figures
plans/              the frozen run plans and their sha256
preregistration/    hashes and copies of the pre-registered plans
skills/             initial skill S0, controls and the frozen candidates
scripts/            run, analysis and probe scripts
tests/
```

All data is synthetic. No real infrastructure or incident records are used.

